    {
      "short": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "title": "The front door, summarised",
      "body": [
        "<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A load balancer gives clients one stable address and spreads requests across servers.</li><li><b>L4</b> forwards connections; <b>L7</b> understands HTTP and can route, terminate TLS and retry.</li><li>Round robin is simple; least connections handles uneven work; hashing keeps a client on one server (consistent hashing for resizes).</li><li>Health checks remove dead servers automatically.</li><li>Run the balancer itself redundantly, or use a managed one.</li></ul>"
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "next",
        "text": "Next: what breaks when a server remembers you — and how to make it forget."
      },
